Urethral Stricture
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Progressive weak/splayed urinary stream, Straining to void, History of urethral trauma/infection, Narrowed fibrotic segment on urethrography

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-047
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe urinary hesitancy, profound straining, and extreme lower abdominal discomfort, requiring urgent assessment for impending urinary retention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient spending hours on the toilet, straining intensely to pass minimal drops of urine, accompanied by progressive lower abdominal pain. Urgent after-hours assessment was critically mandated to measure bladder volume, rule out complete acute urinary retention, and determine if emergency suprapubic catheterization was required before the next available routine clinic appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My urine stream has slowed to a painful trickle, I have to push with all my might just to pass a few drops, and my lower stomach is constantly aching.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a progressive, insidious onset of a markedly weakened, narrowed, and splayed urinary stream over the past six months. Tonight, this escalated severely. They require a prolonged time to completely empty the bladder, relying on intense straining and the use of abdominal musculature to initiate and maintain micturition. They experience a constant sensation of incomplete bladder emptying immediately following voiding, alongside severe post-micturition terminal dribbling. The patient notes a remote history of traumatic urethral catheterization during a surgery five years ago.
Functional Status: Independent, but completely exhausted and functionally limited by the constant, prolonged bathroom visits.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep deprivation and physical exhaustion from straining.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Visibly fatigued, uncomfortable, frequently shifting position due to pelvic pressure.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Mild to moderate suprapubic fullness palpable, dull to percussion, indicating an elevated post-void residual volume, but lacking the tense, agonizing rigidity of absolute complete retention. Genitourinary: External meatus appears normal. Digital rectal examination reveals a normal-sized, smooth, non-tender prostate, firmly ruling out benign prostatic hyperplasia as the primary obstructive cause. Expected investigation results will show a marked reduction in peak urinary flow rate on formal uroflowmetry, alongside direct visualization of a narrowed, fibrotic urethral segment on retrograde urethrography.
Environmental Observations: Bathroom door left open; patient has clearly spent extensive time attempting to void.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications known to cause urinary retention; prior use of over-the-counter saw palmetto yielded zero improvement.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Urethral Stricture - Status: Exacerbated (Impending Retention)
Differentials:

Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the distinctly normal, small prostate on examination and the specific history of traumatic catheterization pointing to urethral scarring.

Acute Prostatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely afebrile presentation, lack of perineal pain, and non-tender prostate.

Neurogenic Bladder - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient has completely normal perineal sensation, intact reflexes, and no history of spinal cord injury.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with near-complete lower urinary tract obstruction driven by dense fibrotic scarring of the urethra (stricture), secondary to past traumatic catheterization. The intense reliance on abdominal straining to force urine through the narrowed lumen has caused severe pelvic fatigue and pain. An urgent after-hours physical examination was absolutely necessary to percuss the bladder, quantify the degree of retention, and safely conclude that while outflow is severely compromised, absolute retention requiring emergency, highly traumatic urethral dilation or suprapubic puncture tonight was not yet present. Deferring care would have left the patient in fear of an impending bladder rupture.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Tamsulosin 400 micrograms stat to maximize any remaining smooth muscle relaxation at the bladder neck, though its efficacy on fixed fibrotic strictures is limited. Generated an urgent, fast-tracked urology referral for formal cystoscopy and likely surgical dilation or urethroplasty. Ordered a formal urinary tract ultrasound with pre and post-void volumes for the morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent dangerous spikes in intravesical pressure and mitigate the risk of bladder wall damage, the patient is instructed to adopt the seated position for all future voiding. Sitting naturally relaxes the pelvic floor musculature, maximizing passive outflow and eliminating the strenuous abdominal pushing used when standing. Experiencing a marginally easier voiding process will positively reinforce this essential postural adjustment.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 48 hours to review the ultrasound report and track the urgent specialist referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department presentation is required if the patient becomes completely unable to pass any urine for more than 6 hours, or if they develop severe, agonizing lower abdominal pain indicating complete obstruction.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical examination strongly suggests a scarred narrowing in the urinary pipe; a specialist must perform a camera test to confirm the exact location and severity.
Clinical Course: The flow will remain extremely slow and difficult until a urologist surgically opens the scarred area. The goal currently is to prevent a complete blockage.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your bladder completely stops emptying, or if the pressure turns into severe, sharp agony, you must go directly to the Emergency Department. Do not allow them to place a standard catheter forcefully, as it will worsen the scar.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A previous medical tube caused scar tissue to form inside your urinary pipe. Over time, this scar has tightened like a noose, making the opening incredibly narrow. Your prostate is perfectly normal. The straining is your bladder frantically trying to push fluid through a pinhole. We must get you to a urologist to surgically stretch or remove this scar.
Actionable Steps: Sit down on the toilet to urinate; do not push or strain forcefully. Drink fluids in small, frequent sips rather than large gulps to avoid rapidly overfilling the bladder.

